\nsl{Cyber-Physical Systems (CPS) are typical mechatronics systems because there are based on mechanical systems augmented with communication capabilities and connectivity controlled
 by IT-components. The basic idea is to take physical systems, described by a set of physical entities and equations and control or monitored these systems by computer based algorithm.
All the components have first to be described and the behavior simulated and during the development process an exchange between the simulated an the real parts take place. 
\newline
Today we find cyber-physical systems especially in bigger projects which deals with aerospace, automotive, chemical, energy, manufacturing applications. CPS involves interdisciplinary approaches 
like system engineering, control theorie but also mechanical design and design in general. In interesting link exsist between CPS and the new technology Internet of Things (IoT).
\newline
In this chapter we'll first try to get a shorter look at cyber-physical systems. Then we focus on a tool which gives as an opportunity to modelling different physical systems 
like mechanical and electrical systems but also question about the thermal requirements we'll try to answer. The modelling tool (Modelica) offers methods to write physical equations or to use a graphical 
user interface to describe systems. The systems itself can have a simple input output behaviour but also complex relationsships.  

}


%==============================================================================
\section{Overview, Delimitation of the Topic}
%==============================================================================
\begin{description}
\item[{\rot Topics of Cyber-Physical Systems}]~\\[-3ex]
\bi
	\ite Combination between: 
	\bi
		\ite mechanical components 
		\ite electrical components
		\ite design aspects
		\ite communication capabilities
		\ite IT-components
	\ei
	\ite simulation of all different components
	\ite study of the dependencies between the components
	\ite mix between simulated components and real hardware (Hardware in the Loop, HIL)
\ei

\defn{def:Cyber-Physical System}
{An \textbf{Cyber-Physical Systems} are integrations of computation with physical processes. Embedded computers and networks monitor and control the physical processes, usually with 
     feedback loops where physical processes affect computations and vice versa. [Edward A. Lee, 2008]}
\os{\newpage}

\begin{figure}[!hbtp]
